<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Bridges P2P channels to the container's loopback proxies, and (eventually) accepts
         inbound WebRTC ICE hole-punch traffic — see design spec §2 "no standing listening ports"
         (ICE binds ephemeral ports; that's not a stable inbound service). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Own bookmark store for non-iCloud sites (design spec §2 "File access") — the main app's
         bookmarks are app-scoped and don't transfer to this bundle ID. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Apple Containerization framework (#69): boot local OCI containers, same as Anglesite.app. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.virtualization</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Shared keychain access group (#1208 P2): lets this helper and Anglesite.app read the same
         SecretAccounts.devicePairingKey item, so this process signs signaling payloads with the
         very key the main app's Settings ▸ Devices pane publishes in its pairing QR code. Must
         match Resources/Anglesite.entitlements' identical array exactly, and must match the
         expanded form KeychainStore.sharedPairingAccessGroup carries at runtime
         ("M34HBJZNYA.io.dwk.anglesite.shared") — $(AppIdentifierPrefix) is a build-time
         substitution that nothing expands at runtime. A mismatch is silent: each process simply
         keeps its own item and pairing never verifies, which is why the runtime string lives in
         one Swift constant instead of being written out at each call site.
         Keychain Sharing needs no Apple Developer portal capability (every App ID carries it
         implicitly), but the entitlement still requires a provisioning profile to sign, so this
         key can only live in a Release/real-Team file — see step 2 of the note below for the
         measurement and Resources/AnglesiteRemote-Debug.entitlements for the CI-safe Debug
         counterpart this target now has. -->
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)io.dwk.anglesite.shared</string>
    </array>

    <!-- NO com.apple.developer.icloud-container-identifiers and NO
         com.apple.security.application-groups here yet — both require a provisioning profile
         even under ad-hoc/Manual signing (see Resources/Anglesite-Debug.entitlements' identical
         note re: #1038). Task 6 Step 5 documents the manual Apple Developer portal step that
         unblocks adding them.

         NOTE this is now the *Release* entitlements file for the AnglesiteRemote target. It no
         longer has to keep the CI-safe, no-Team-required shape: Debug builds sign against
         $(ANGLESITE_REMOTE_DEBUG_ENTITLEMENTS), defaulting to
         Resources/AnglesiteRemote-Debug.entitlements. Adding either capability above here would
         therefore not break the no-Apple-account Debug build — but both still need their portal
         step first, so they stay out.

         STEPS for #1208 P2. Steps 1 and 3 are Apple Developer portal steps (Team KH7H8Y25RT) that
         cannot be performed from this repo — each capability has to be enabled on the App ID in
         the portal first, and only then added here. Step 2 was never a portal step and is now
         DONE; its note is kept as the record of what was measured and what it does and does not
         buy.

         1. CloudKit signaling. Add to THIS file:
              com.apple.developer.icloud-container-identifiers = [iCloud.io.dwk.anglesite]
              com.apple.developer.icloud-services            = [CloudKit]
            and add "CloudKit" to the existing com.apple.developer.icloud-services array in
            Resources/Anglesite.entitlements, which today lists only CloudDocuments (enough for
            iCloud Drive site storage, not for CloudKit).
            Until that lands, the helper's `connect` invocation degrades to a local directory
            mailbox for the signaling transport. It does NOT construct any CloudKit type: this
            process checks its own code signature first, because CKContainer(identifier:) traps
            the process (SIGTRAP) rather than failing when the entitlement is absent. See
            `processCarriesCloudKitEntitlement(containerIdentifier:)` in
            Sources/anglesite-remote-helper/main.swift. Signing and key pinning stay live in the
            degraded mode, so only cross-network rendezvous is lost.

         2. Shared keychain access group — DONE (2026-08-14). Kept as a record, because what was
            measured here constrains anyone who touches these files next.

            The problem it solved: the helper signs signaling payloads with a DevicePairingKeyPair
            read from the Keychain, but Keychain items are scoped per bundle ID, so this helper
            could not read the key the main app's Settings pane generated and published in its
            pairing QR code, and minted its own instead. The keychain-access-groups entitlement
            above, and its twin in Resources/Anglesite.entitlements, is what makes the two the
            same device identity.

            CORRECTION: this step used to open with an Apple Developer portal sub-step, on the
            assumption that Keychain Sharing needed a portal capability the way CloudKit and App
            Groups do. It does not — every App ID carries Keychain Sharing implicitly. Removing
            that false blocker uncovered a real one, measured rather than assumed: adding

                <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
                <array><string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)io.dwk.anglesite.shared</string></array>

            to a file the ad-hoc Debug build signs against fails that build outright with

                error: "AnglesiteRemote" requires a provisioning profile. Enable development
                signing and select a provisioning profile in the Signing & Capabilities editor.

            Removing those four lines makes the same build succeed; substituting a literal
            "M34HBJZNYA.io.dwk.anglesite.shared" for the $(AppIdentifierPrefix) form fails
            identically, so it is the entitlement itself and not the variable expansion. So the
            portal half of the assumption was wrong but the signing half was not: as far as
            codesign is concerned keychain-access-groups sits with the iCloud and Associated
            Domains entitlements the top of this block describes, needing a real provisioning
            profile that ad-hoc/no-Team signing cannot self-grant. It differs from them only in
            that no portal capability has to be enabled first.

            Which is why the fix was a project-configuration split, mirroring what the Anglesite
            and AnglesiteMobile targets already do:
              - Resources/AnglesiteRemote-Debug.entitlements (new) — the CI-safe default, no
                keychain group, so a clone with no Apple Developer account still builds Debug.
              - xcconfig/Signing-Debug.xcconfig gained
                ANGLESITE_REMOTE_DEBUG_ENTITLEMENTS = Resources/AnglesiteRemote-Debug.entitlements
              - project.yml's AnglesiteRemote target gained
                configs.Debug.CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS = $(ANGLESITE_REMOTE_DEBUG_ENTITLEMENTS);
                its base value (THIS file) is now the Release value.
              - Resources/AnglesiteRemote-Debug-Keychain.entitlements (new) — opt-in Debug variant
                carrying the group, for a contributor with a real Team, selected from a gitignored
                xcconfig/Signing-Debug.local.xcconfig. Mirrors Anglesite-Debug-iCloud.entitlements.

            What that does and does not buy. Sharing is live in Release/real-Team builds. In the
            DEFAULT Debug build neither bundle carries the group, so both pairing-key call sites
            get errSecMissingEntitlement from SecItem and degrade loudly rather than silently:
            the Settings pane reports "couldn't prepare pairing key" instead of rendering a QR,
            and the helper logs the ephemeral-key warning. That is the accepted cost of keeping
            the no-Apple-account Debug build working; a local Debug run that needs the real
            behavior opts in via the two -Keychain / -iCloud entitlements files above.

            Also note KeychainStore.sharedPairingAccessGroup's team prefix was corrected to
            M34HBJZNYA (the team whose Apple Development certificate signs local builds) from an
            earlier assumed KH7H8Y25RT; KeychainStoreTests pins its exact shape.
            $(AppIdentifierPrefix) is a build-time substitution that nothing expands at runtime,
            which is why that Swift constant carries the team prefix literally and must be kept in
            step with whatever team ends up signing. Sources/AnglesiteCore/Platform/SecretStore.swift's
            readDevicePairingKeyPair()/writeDevicePairingKeyPair() needed no change — verified
            store-agnostic, operating on whatever KeychainStore instance they are handed.

            STILL OWNER-LOCAL, the one thing no build here can settle: whether the two processes
            actually share one Keychain item. Launch the app, generate the QR in Settings ▸
            Devices, then confirm the helper logs no "generated this helper's device pairing key"
            line on a subsequent cold start — meaning it found the app's existing key rather than
            minting its own. Delete both processes' pre-existing per-bundle keys first, or the
            stale ones mask the result. Use a Release build, or a Debug build with both opt-in
            entitlements files selected; a default Debug build cannot show this.

         3. Ubiquity container. RemoteSiteResolver's zero-prompt iCloud path stays dormant until
            this bundle ID carries the iCloud container entitlement from step 1; every site
            resolution takes the one-time access-panel/bookmark path in the meantime. That is
            already documented on RemoteSiteResolver itself. -->
</dict>
</plist>
